another fruitless visit to Baghdad  Central Criminal Court a bus and without  Sun Ahmed is a tuk-tuk driver and was  detained in February while taking part  in anti-government protests the charges  against my son is that he blocked the  roads he didn't throw petrol bombs he  didn't even carry a mobile he's a  peaceful protester demanding his rights  I don't know why he isn't being released  he has done nothing wrong I just want to  know his fate the judge has twice  refused to release Ahmed on bail but his  parents hoped he would finally walk free  after iraq's newly appointed prime  minister Mustafa Al Queda me promised to  release detained protesters but what was  supposed to be a decisive move to mend  drifts with the protest movement has  turned into a political row Council  announced it would only release  protesters if they had not committed any  crimes but that statement seems to be at  odds with the new prime minister's  orders who said that all demonstrators  should be released except if they were  involved in bloodshed this has led many  to question the Prime Minister's  authority to bring about change it's a  political game the Prime Minister  ordered to release the protesters but  the judiciary's under the control of the  political parties there are powerful  parties controlling the judiciary  according to the Constitution the Prime  Minister has limited power over judges  he can give advice to relieve the  tension on the ground to facilitate the  judiciary processes otherwise if we have  anyone committed any crime or he have  been charged of course he cannot and  from the start we say that if we have  anyone who have been tried already or  charged it needs special amnesty law  adding more confusion the judiciary  issued a statement on Wednesday claiming  that no peaceful protesters are being  detained in Iraq prisons this has led a  bus and we dare to fear even more for  their son's fate they meet a lawyer in  Tahrir Square alongside several other  families  esra Mohammed represents 15 detained  demonstrators none have been released  since Academy's announcement Omar :  they're not complicated cases all of  them are charged under the same articles  which is attacking security forces with  petrol bombs and vandalism the delay in  delivering on his promises risks  undermining the new prime minister's  efforts to win over the public it is  also a sign of the challenges ahead as  he sets out to implement his program  amid a deeply fractured political  landscape semana 14 al Jazeera Baghdad
 

